.TH "UBERTOOTH\-DUCKY" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-ducky\fR \- drive an Uberducky to act like a USB Rubber Ducky
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-ducky [\-q [uuid]] [\-b] [\-A index] [\-a BD_ADDR] [\-s]
[\-U <index|serial>]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Companion tool for Uberducky, which uses an Ubertooth to inject BLE HID ("Rubber Ducky"\-style keystroke) payloads\. See the Uberducky project for the payload format and setup:
.IP "" 4
.nf
https://github\.com/mikeryan/uberducky
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
If no Bluetooth address is given, a random BD_ADDR is used (read from /dev/urandom)\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-q [uuid]\fR
quack \(em trigger the configured payload (optional target uuid)\.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR
signal the Uberducky to enter bootloader (DFU) mode\.
.TP
\fB\-A <37|38|39>\fR
advertising channel index (default: 38)\.
.TP
\fB\-a <BD_ADDR>\fR
Bluetooth address to use, \fBxx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR (default: random)\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
show help\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Injection advertises at the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leaves it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level transmits at 7 (0 dBm), the CC2400's reset value\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-ducky\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: advertising channel index must be 37\-39 (got 40)\fR\.
.P
The optional value of \fB\-q\fR may be attached or given as the next word (\fB\-q <uuid>\fR); a following option is never taken as a value\.
.P
\fB\-q\fR or \fB\-b\fR is the work, so a command line with neither is refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: nothing to do: give \-q to quack, or \-b to signal the bootloader\fR\. It used to open the selected board, check its firmware, quack nothing and exit 0\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
What \fBubertooth\-ducky\fR does happens over the air, so it writes no result: everything it prints, including the duck and the "Quacking \|\.\|\.\|\. as \|\.\|\.\|\." line, goes to standard error, and standard output stays empty\. Each error is one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: cannot read the UUID 'nonsense'\fR\.
.P
A request the board refuses ends the command there, named in one line, for example \fBerror: could not set the advertising channel\fR; libubertooth has already said why\. A refused request is not proof that the board never got it, so the message does not claim one way or the other\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-U' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\. A bare \fBubertooth\-ducky\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR: for \fB\-q\fR and \fB\-b\fR, when the board took every request the advertisement needs\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take or with nothing to do, for a UUID it cannot read, when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened, and when the board refuses one of those requests\.
.P
1 as well when the board refuses the request that stops the advertisement at the end of the two seconds: the board is left advertising, and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
